        "abstract": "We can look at a first-order (or propositional) intuitionistic Kripke model as an ordered set of classical models. In this paper, we show that for a finite-depth Kripke model in an arbitrary first-order language or propositional language, local (classical) truth of a formula is equivalent to non-classical truth (truth in the Kripke semantics) of a Friedman's translation of that formula, i.e. <tex xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">$ \\alpha \\Vdash A^\\rho \\Leftrightarrow \\mathfrak{M}_\\alpha \\models A$</tex>. We introduce some applications of this fact. We extend the result of Ardeshir and Hesaam (2002, Math. Logic Quart., 48, 391–395) and show that semi-narrow Kripke models of Heyting Arithmetic <tex xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">$ \\textsf{HA} $</tex> are locally <tex xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">$ \\textsf{PA} $</tex>.",
        "keywords": {
